49.8 A sub-expression is a value
Writing a sentence as plain function calls makes one law easy to see. A piece of a sentence is an ordinary JavaScript value, so you can give it a name, use it again, and pass it to a function. It means the same thing in every sentence that holds it:


const piled = layer(point, bin, stack);
console.log(String(plot(data(gapminder_2007), piled, x(col.life))));
&lt;gog plot: point on data&gt;


Printing a plot gives one line, naming its mark and its table, here the generated name data. It does not give the picture. piled could go into a second sentence unchanged, and those three words would mean the same thing there. That law is Compositional Invariance, the sixth of the nine laws.

49.9 A plot for a web page: html_block()
Everything so far has been about writing a sentence. The rest of this chapter is about using one: on a web page, with a table from a database, and from an installed package.
render_svg() returns a plot as an SVG string: the drawing and nothing else. For a web page, html_block() returns the same drawing with the buttons under every plot. Some plots also get a copy of the engine compiled for the browser, which redraws the plot as you turn or brush it. Four kinds of plot need it: a plot in the cube, a plot on the globe, a network given a viewing angle, and a plot with a brush. Those are the plots of Space, Globe, Network and Selection.


const cube = plot(data(gapminder_2007), point,
                  x(col.gdp), y(col.life), z(col.population));
console.log(html_block(cube).split("\n")[0]);
&lt;div class="gog-plot" id="gog-a2983707b2" style="text-align:center;"&gt;


The function returns HTML as a string. Put it in a page, a notebook cell, or a dashboard panel. The drawing comes first in that string, so a reader sees the plot before the copy of the engine has finished loading. That copy is compiled to WebAssembly, and a page whose security policy forbids WebAssembly still shows the plot. What stops working is everything that copy does. The cube, the globe and a network given a viewing angle no longer turn, and a drag on a brushed plot selects nothing. The buttons under every plot still work, because they do not need WebAssembly.
R, Python and Julia never call this function. A notebook in those languages asks the plot object how it would be displayed, and each binding answers: R with repr_html, Python with _repr_html_, Julia with a show method. JavaScript has no such request to answer, so here it is a function you call.
A script run outside a notebook has nowhere to put that HTML, so show(plot) saves it to a file in your computer’s folder for temporary files. It returns the file’s path. Unlike Python’s plot.show(), it opens no browser.
Three more functions are for building a tool with gog, such as a web server, and never for writing a sentence. svg_block(svg) puts an SVG string in centered HTML that shrinks to fit a web page, without the controls. find_gog_cli() returns the path of the engine the package found (Installing gog). to_wire(table, name) returns the table as gog sends it to the engine, before it becomes JSON; name labels its messages. Julia exports these three as well; R and Python do not.

49.10 Reading from a database
Your table may be stored in a database rather than held in your session. R, Python and Julia each have a database standard, but Node.js, the program that runs JavaScript outside a browser, has none. So query() accepts a connection by the methods it has rather than by its type. Any connection will work if it can prepare a statement and return all its rows:
import { DatabaseSync } from "node:sqlite";
import { plot, data, query, bar, x, y, col, render_svg } from "grammar-of-graphics";

const con = new DatabaseSync("sales.db");
render_svg(plot(query(con, "SELECT status, revenue FROM orders"),
  bar, x(col.status), y(col.revenue)));
node:sqlite is built into Node.js. It works without a flag from Node.js 22.13, and from 23.4 in the version 23 series, so on those versions that example needs nothing installed. Node.js prints a warning that the module is experimental, and the plot is drawn anyway. better-sqlite3 has the same shape and works the same way.

49.10.1 Asynchronous drivers
Not every driver, the package that connects to one kind of database, returns its rows that way. render_svg() is synchronous: it returns the SVG rather than a Promise. That is what lets a plot be written inline in an expression. A driver whose query returns a Promise cannot be awaited inside a synchronous function.
So pg and mysql2 do not work with query(), and gog says so rather than letting a Promise reach the engine. Fetch the rows yourself instead. Both drivers return an array of rows, one object per row, and data() takes that array directly:
const { rows } = await con.query("SELECT status, revenue FROM orders");
render_svg(plot(data(rows), bar, x(col.status), y(col.revenue)));
The first line is written for pg. With mysql2 it begins const [rows] = instead. The line after it is the sentence you would write for any table. The driver also decides each column’s type: pg returns a count or a numeric column as text, so cast it in the SQL, as in COUNT(*)::int or revenue::float8. Everywhere else, a rule of JavaScript changes only how a sentence is spelled. Here it changes what a sentence can do: the query runs before the sentence, with one await.
